WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.300
I'm in the English countryside near Canterbury,

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.380
and this is a level crossing.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.540
Nothing too unusual about that, except this

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.280
is a user operated level crossing.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.480
And they are fairly few and far between.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.300
This little red and green light here tells you when it's safe

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.920
to cross and then when it is you have to get out

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.100
of the car that you drove up here in, and you have to

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.260
physically pump the barriers to get them to rise.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.200
I'm aware quite how dodgy this movement looks.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.280
And this in itself is not too unusual, you know,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.780
there are crossings like this in odd regional parts of England,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.660
in the valleys of Wales, on smaller lines, but the thing

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.940
you might not know is that this is not a provincial line

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:48.500
This is a high speed rail line that has Javelin trains on the London to Ramsgate run.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.400
The ones that do 140mph.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.880
And, okay, they're not doing 140 here,

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.420
they're doing about 40, maybe 50mph at most,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.780
but all that's stopping me from blocking this line,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.520
or worse, being hit by something on this line, is -- well -- this crossing

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.720
and this little lever down here, that drops

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.460
the barriers and makes it safe for the trains

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.260
to cross again. That is a user operated level crossing

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.320
and that is something you might not have known.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.240
